<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seth was an ancient Egyptian god of storms, the desert, foreign lands and disorder. In myth he murdered his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> and fought Osiris's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> for the throne, but he also defended the sun god against the serpent Apophis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> rulers honoured him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>, and the Ramesside kings, among them Seti I, took their names from him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Set was the son of Geb, the Earth, and Nut, the Sky, and his siblings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> and Nephthys; he married Nephthys. In art he is usually shown as an animal that Egyptologists call the Set animal, which has not been identified with any known species. It has a downward curving snout, long ears with squared-off ends and a thin, forked tail. Proposed identities include the Saluki, aardvark, hyena, donkey and giraffe. The earliest certain Set animal appears on a ceremonial macehead of Scorpion II, a ruler of the Naqada III phase. The meaning of his name is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Set was lord of the Red Land, the desert, balancing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> as lord of the Black Land, the fertile land. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> myth he murders and mutilates his brother Osiris. Isis reassembles the body, and Osiris lives long enough to father Horus, who seeks revenge. The conflict appears as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a>, where the two gods are sometimes uncle and nephew and sometimes brothers. In the account called Contendings, the gods race in boats and fight as hippopotami before an assembly of deities, and the dispute lasts eighty years, largely because the judge favours Set. Horus loses an eye to Set, and the restored eye represents the return of the moon to full brightness and of kingship to Horus.</p><p>Set also stood on the prow of Ra's barge and helped overcome the serpent Apep. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a> (1650 to 1550 BCE) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a>, took him as patron, identifying him with their own god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> expelled the Hyksos, the cult at Avaris continued. Ramesses I came from a military family from Avaris with ties to Set's priesthood, and several Ramesside kings took names from the god, among them Seti I and Setnakht. Ramesses II set up the Year 400 Stela at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a> for the cult's 400th anniversary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Egyptologist Herman te Velde holds that Set was demonised after foreign conquests in the Third Intermediate and Late Periods, when the god of foreigners became linked with the Kushite and Persian rulers. His murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> was emphasised and his defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> welcomed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> later associated him with Typhon. Even so, in outlying places such as Kharga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakhla">Dakhla</a> he remained a chief deity, called Lord of the Oasis, and he was worshipped at Ombos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>. Today he is honoured in Kemetism, a revival of ancient Egyptian religion, and in the Temple of Set, founded in 1975.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Set (deity)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From around the 24th century BCE the god Osiris, a king murdered by his brother <b>Seth</b> and restored to life by his sister and wife Isis, rose to great importance as lord of the dead.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The army fought in Nubia and the Levant: Ramesses II marched to the Battle of Kadesh in 1274 BCE with four divisions named after the gods Amun, Ra, Ptah and <b>Seth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>…Hussain Khan Bandanawazi, Farid Ayaz and Abu Muhammad Qawwal, Jafar Hussain Khan, the sitar and sarod players Ustad Vilayat Khan and Ustad Shujaat Khan, Zila Khan, the Nizami Bandhu, Hadiqa Kiani, Smita Bellur, Lata Mangeshkar, Asha Bhosle, Rahat Fateh Ali Khan, Kailash Kher, Kavita <b>Seth</b> and Maithili Thakur.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chhaap-tilak-sab-chheeni">Chhaap Tilak Sab Chheeni</a></li><li><blockquote>The Second Dynasty saw unrest, with Peribsen adopting <b>Seth</b> rather than Horus as his patron, before Khasekhemwy reunited the country.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>They worshipped <b>Seth</b>, identified with the storm god Baal, and traded widely with Canaan and Cyprus.</blockquote><a href="/state/fifteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Fifteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>The best-attested king, Nehesy, whose name means 'the Nubian', left monuments at Avaris and Tell el-Muqdam and honoured <b>Seth</b> as lord of Avaris.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Fourteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
